{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And there was a king in Jeshurun.</b> Whether it refers to the Holy One, blessed be He, or to Moses, this is its meaning: for Jeshurun is a term of seeing, and it is the way of the world that sometimes a man rises to greatness even if he is not worthy of it, because people do not look at him carefully to see what his true nature is — therefore they leave him in his greatness, for 'they have eyes but do not see' after him what his quality is. But if they were to look and see into his substance — that there is nothing real in him — they would bring him down from his greatness. But Moses is not so: even though it is said (Exodus 33:8), ״וְהִבִּיטוּ אַחֲרֵי מֹשֶׁה״ — that they would scrutinize after him — nevertheless they found in him not the slightest flaw. Therefore ״וַיְהִי בִישֻׁרוּן מֶלֶךְ״ — he was king even among those who scrutinize. And in this manner it is also explained concerning the kingship of Shaddai, for they were always casting doubt after Him through many trials, and in all of them they found that He, blessed be He, is their king."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:21.276146543Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}